WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.601
-Our next guest is an Emmy
and Peabody-winning

00:00:02.601 --> 00:00:04.067
writer and producer
whose work, you know,

00:00:04.067 --> 00:00:06.601
from shows like "Inside Amy
Schumer," "Big Mouth,"

00:00:06.601 --> 00:00:09.133
And "I Love That for You."
Her best-selling book,

00:00:09.133 --> 00:00:12.300
"I'll Show Myself Out: Essays
on Midlife and Motherhood,"

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.934
is out now in paperback.
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:14.934 --> 00:00:16.300
Jessi Klein, everybody.

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:17.634
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:17.634 --> 00:00:24.968
♪♪

00:00:24.968 --> 00:00:32.300
♪♪

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:33.501
Welcome back, Jessi.

00:00:33.501 --> 00:00:35.467
-Thank you.
-We were talking backstage

00:00:35.467 --> 00:00:37.667
the last time you were here
was a live show.

00:00:37.667 --> 00:00:39.334
-Yes.
-We were on late at night

00:00:39.334 --> 00:00:42.601
in 2016, the evening
that Hillary Clinton

00:00:42.601 --> 00:00:44.968
accepted the Democratic
nomination for president.

00:00:44.968 --> 00:00:46.501
-Yeah.
-And pretty much all we talked

00:00:46.501 --> 00:00:48.534
about were how many balloons
dropped from the ceiling.

00:00:48.534 --> 00:00:49.901
-There were so many balloons.

00:00:49.901 --> 00:00:52.334
We talked about
just how excited and happy

00:00:52.334 --> 00:00:54.267
we were that everything
was about to be perfect.

00:00:54.267 --> 00:00:55.534
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:00:55.534 --> 00:00:57.434
-We didn't realize the
trajectory of the balloons

00:00:57.434 --> 00:00:59.167
was downward.
-Was down through the floor

00:00:59.167 --> 00:01:02.801
into full hell. Yeah.
That they were lead balloons.

00:01:02.801 --> 00:01:04.200
-They were lead balloons,
it turns out.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:06.400
-Yeah.
-This is a wonderful book

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:08.334
of essays, which we when it
first came out,

00:01:08.334 --> 00:01:10.968
we talked about it a little bit.

00:01:10.968 --> 00:01:13.067
We were writers together at
"SNL."

00:01:13.067 --> 00:01:14.868
And you're very honest
about a step you took

00:01:14.868 --> 00:01:18.567
to sort of deal with the stress
and pressure of the table

00:01:18.567 --> 00:01:20.067
read on Wednesday.
-Yes.

00:01:20.067 --> 00:01:22.267
-And it was lovely to read it
because it was a reminder

00:01:22.267 --> 00:01:24.801
that there's just no way
to make it easier.

00:01:24.801 --> 00:01:26.501
-Well, you're being very kind,
Seth,

00:01:26.501 --> 00:01:27.901
because what people here
don't know is

00:01:27.901 --> 00:01:30.801
that I just bombed the whole
year that I wrote for "SNL,"

00:01:30.801 --> 00:01:33.300
and it's a very stressful table
read and everyone's there,

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:36.634
and I was trying to figure out
what could I do

00:01:36.634 --> 00:01:40.000
to make up for my lack of talent
in writing on this show?

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:41.200
And I decided the thing to do

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:44.100
was to get a lot of, like,
good luck jewelry

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:47.501
that had, like, inspirational
things engraved on it

00:01:47.501 --> 00:01:49.934
because that would project
a lot of confidence.

00:01:49.934 --> 00:01:51.267
[ Laughter ]

00:01:51.267 --> 00:01:53.534
And so I remember
I bought a necklace

00:01:53.534 --> 00:01:56.234
that said "fearlessness" on it
because I'm like,

00:01:56.234 --> 00:01:58.968
if people see that, they won't
realize these sketches suck.

00:01:58.968 --> 00:02:00.534
[ Laughter ]

00:02:00.534 --> 00:02:04.167
And then I bought on Etsy,
which is a great website

00:02:04.167 --> 00:02:06.934
for engravable
inspirational jewelry,

00:02:06.934 --> 00:02:11.501
I bought a ring that said --
it said on the inside,

00:02:11.501 --> 00:02:14.334
"This too shall pass."
-Okay.

00:02:14.334 --> 00:02:16.267
-And so I would be
in that table read

00:02:16.267 --> 00:02:18.834
waiting for my sketches
to be horribly over

00:02:18.834 --> 00:02:23.067
and just be like, "This too,
the sketch too shall pass."

00:02:23.067 --> 00:02:25.367
And then they would,
into oblivion.

00:02:25.367 --> 00:02:28.167
-In the end, do you feel like it
was worth -- the money

00:02:28.167 --> 00:02:29.701
was worth it on
the inspirational jewelry?

00:02:29.701 --> 00:02:31.467
-Absolutely not.
A full waste.

00:02:31.467 --> 00:02:33.667
Yeah. Money into the garbage.

00:02:33.667 --> 00:02:35.901
-Yeah.
-"Edible Boyfriend," right?

00:02:35.901 --> 00:02:37.133
-Wait, what?

00:02:37.133 --> 00:02:39.801
-"Edible Boyfriend" was
a Jessi Klein sketch.

00:02:39.801 --> 00:02:42.334
-Oh, yeah. I was like -- Yes.

00:02:42.334 --> 00:02:45.501
-That is a crazy thing
to just say out of nowhere.

00:02:45.501 --> 00:02:47.100
I do apologize
for no context.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:48.934
-I had a moment of, like,
needing out of the car.

00:02:48.934 --> 00:02:51.367
-"Edible Boyfriend" and you're
like, "Wait, is that a story

00:02:51.367 --> 00:02:52.601
I'm supposed to tell?"

00:02:52.601 --> 00:02:55.300
Now, you had a boyfriend once
you ate, right?

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:59.267
-Yes, I just finished
eating my boyfriend.

00:02:59.267 --> 00:03:00.334
I'm like, "This appearance
went so well."

00:03:00.334 --> 00:03:02.033
-I hope my memory's right.
Yes?

00:03:02.033 --> 00:03:03.767
-Well, I think the confusion

00:03:03.767 --> 00:03:05.067
and the reason
I just got whiplash

00:03:05.067 --> 00:03:06.701
was it was called
"Brownie Husband."

00:03:06.701 --> 00:03:08.767
-Okay. Gotcha.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:08.767 --> 00:03:10.801
-But it could have been called

00:03:10.801 --> 00:03:13.667
and should have been called
"Edible Boyfriend."

00:03:13.667 --> 00:03:15.734
-I will say
as a talk show host,

00:03:15.734 --> 00:03:17.934
I have done you no favors.
-No, no, no.

00:03:17.934 --> 00:03:20.501
You are doing me the
opposite of dirty.

00:03:20.501 --> 00:03:22.200
-Alright. Thanks.
-Yeah.

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:24.467
-I sent one of your essays
to my wife,

00:03:24.467 --> 00:03:26.100
who then, over the course
of the next week --

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.467
-Edible wife.
-Yeah, my edible wife.

00:03:28.467 --> 00:03:29.601
-Edible wife.

00:03:29.601 --> 00:03:31.033
-She used to be
my brownie girlfriend.

00:03:31.033 --> 00:03:35.167
I made her an honest edible.
-An honest edible.

00:03:35.167 --> 00:03:36.701
-But she, you know,

00:03:36.701 --> 00:03:38.367
she said that in the course
of the week that followed,

00:03:38.367 --> 00:03:40.200
she said like eight other moms
had sent it to her.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.634
-Yeah.
-And I feel like you must be

00:03:42.634 --> 00:03:43.834
getting feedback like that.

00:03:43.834 --> 00:03:46.234
-Yeah. It was really nice.

00:03:46.234 --> 00:03:51.267
Yeah, the first chapter
in the book kind of went viral.

00:03:51.267 --> 00:03:53.400
It was like published
in "New York Magazine,"

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:56.167
and I just started
getting a lot of

00:03:56.167 --> 00:03:59.000
like -- a lot of women
sliding into my DMs,

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:02.667
which is really what you
want, a lot of moms,

00:04:02.667 --> 00:04:05.567
but just saying like,
"I feel seen,

00:04:05.567 --> 00:04:07.501
thank you for writing this."
And then there was sometimes

00:04:07.501 --> 00:04:11.200
just a level of like
sort of desperation

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:12.767
that you would feel of like,

00:04:12.767 --> 00:04:16.534
"I have like three kids
under the age of five and, like,

00:04:16.534 --> 00:04:18.534
I'm at the bottom
of the ocean, no one's slept."

00:04:18.534 --> 00:04:20.567
And you're like,
"Maybe you should call 911."

00:04:20.567 --> 00:04:22.334
-Yeah.
-Instead of DMing me.

00:04:22.334 --> 00:04:24.367
But I was very grateful.
It was really nice.

00:04:24.367 --> 00:04:26.534
-I should note one of
the important things to say

00:04:26.534 --> 00:04:28.334
about this book
is it's wildly funny,

00:04:28.334 --> 00:04:30.767
and one of the things
I've heard you say is like,

00:04:30.767 --> 00:04:32.467
it's really hard.

00:04:32.467 --> 00:04:34.033
You don't really want
to hear about people --

00:04:34.033 --> 00:04:36.901
how great people's kids are,
but it's super fun to hear

00:04:36.901 --> 00:04:39.534
that other people's kids
are pains in the asses.

00:04:39.534 --> 00:04:41.167
-Oh, all I want to do

00:04:41.167 --> 00:04:44.834
is hear about what turds
other people's kids are.

00:04:44.834 --> 00:04:46.634
Yeah, there's nothing worse
than, like,

00:04:46.634 --> 00:04:49.834
someone else's, like, polite,
well-behaved kid

00:04:49.834 --> 00:04:51.634
who eats everything
they're supposed to do.

00:04:51.634 --> 00:04:53.834
Like, I remember when my kid

00:04:53.834 --> 00:04:55.334
first started
going to preschool,

00:04:55.334 --> 00:04:58.968
I would see what other kids
had in their lunch boxes

00:04:58.968 --> 00:05:00.501
because, like,
my kid's still --

00:05:00.501 --> 00:05:02.701
He's almost eight and he only
eats, like, bread and cheese,

00:05:02.701 --> 00:05:06.133
which is, of course, what we all
want to eat and what I eat now

00:05:06.133 --> 00:05:07.767
because I'm an adult
and I can do whatever I want.

00:05:07.767 --> 00:05:10.234
But there was like those kids

00:05:10.234 --> 00:05:11.834
who have those
little bento boxes.

00:05:11.834 --> 00:05:14.534
Like, there was -- I remember
a kid who had just

00:05:14.534 --> 00:05:16.100
cherry tomatoes,

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:17.968
raw cherry tomatoes,
in one of the sections.

00:05:17.968 --> 00:05:19.667
I saw this kid
eat the cherry tomato,

00:05:19.667 --> 00:05:22.167
and I was like,
"I have to go punch a wall."

00:05:22.167 --> 00:05:23.934
I'm so -- How dare you?

00:05:23.934 --> 00:05:26.267
How dare you? All of you.

00:05:26.267 --> 00:05:28.634
-So, I actually have met
this person as well.

00:05:28.634 --> 00:05:29.801
I don't know
if it's the same person,

00:05:29.801 --> 00:05:31.801
but you write an essay
about the car seat lady,

00:05:31.801 --> 00:05:33.334
which I think might --
some people

00:05:33.334 --> 00:05:35.067
might not believe
is a real thing.

00:05:35.067 --> 00:05:36.334
-Yeah, she's real.

00:05:36.334 --> 00:05:37.601
And I bet it probably
is the same car seat lady.

00:05:37.601 --> 00:05:39.100
-I think so, too.
I feel like she may be,

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:41.133
like, just, you know,
copyrighted the name.

00:05:41.133 --> 00:05:43.434
-Yeah. There's --
When you have a child,

00:05:43.434 --> 00:05:45.367
by law, you have to put them
into a car seat.

00:05:45.367 --> 00:05:46.534
-Yeah.
-To keep them safe.

00:05:46.534 --> 00:05:48.400
Unlike when we were kids
and you would just, like,

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:51.367
rattle around in the back of
whatever.

00:05:51.367 --> 00:05:52.767
But yeah,
there's a car seat lady.

00:05:52.767 --> 00:05:55.267
You hear about her through,
like, word of mouth.

00:05:55.267 --> 00:05:56.934
Sort of like a drug dealer.

00:05:56.934 --> 00:05:59.033
And then she -- You meet her
on a corner.

00:05:59.033 --> 00:06:01.133
-For real you do.
-You meet her on a corner.

00:06:01.133 --> 00:06:02.868
-You bring a car
and a car seat.

00:06:02.868 --> 00:06:05.734
-You bring a car and a car seat
and you meet her on a corner

00:06:05.734 --> 00:06:08.400
and she's just like,
"You want a car seat?"

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:10.400
Because they're
very hard to install.

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:12.734
-It's the dumbest thing
in the world.

00:06:12.801 --> 00:06:14.167
-It's the dumbest thing
in the world.

00:06:14.167 --> 00:06:16.601
And it's very exacting
how you have to do it

00:06:16.601 --> 00:06:17.968
or at least she makes you
feel --

00:06:17.968 --> 00:06:20.334
I mean, if she's watching,
thank you for your service.

00:06:20.334 --> 00:06:21.501
-Oh, my God.

00:06:21.501 --> 00:06:23.601
I want to make it
very clear as well.

00:06:23.601 --> 00:06:25.133
Yes. Once she left,
I was like,

00:06:25.133 --> 00:06:27.567
thank God,
we have avoided disaster.

00:06:27.567 --> 00:06:28.734
-Yeah, we've avoided disaster.

00:06:28.734 --> 00:06:30.434
But she's like,
"If you have this,"

00:06:30.434 --> 00:06:32.167
I mean, it's
a five-point harness.

00:06:32.167 --> 00:06:33.501
-Yeah.
-And if she's like,

00:06:33.501 --> 00:06:35.400
"If you have it a centimeter up
or a centimeter down

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:38.000
in the wrong place,
like, you might as well

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:40.434
just throw your kid
into the trash."

00:06:40.434 --> 00:06:42.267
[ Laughter ]

00:06:42.267 --> 00:06:44.534
Yeah, that's it. Yeah.

00:06:44.534 --> 00:06:47.801
And I did meet one
at a parenting class.

00:06:47.801 --> 00:06:48.901
I remember I met this dad

00:06:48.901 --> 00:06:50.667
who also had had
the same car seat lady

00:06:50.667 --> 00:06:52.801
and we were talking about how
bad she makes us feel

00:06:52.801 --> 00:06:55.100
about what happens
if we F it up.

00:06:55.100 --> 00:06:58.834
And he was saying that when
she was installing his car seat,

00:06:58.834 --> 00:07:00.634
she was like,
"What's this on the floor?"

00:07:00.634 --> 00:07:03.767
And he had a loose
meat cleaver...

00:07:03.767 --> 00:07:05.634
[ Laughter ]

00:07:05.634 --> 00:07:09.200
...that had fallen out of like
a West Elm box of knives

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:11.767
he'd bought and he never knew
where it went.

00:07:11.767 --> 00:07:17.367
So he was the dad who just
had, like, a giant knife.

00:07:17.367 --> 00:07:18.567
Yeah. I was like,
"At least I'm not that guy."

00:07:18.567 --> 00:07:20.234
-She's like, "This probably goes
without saying,

00:07:20.234 --> 00:07:21.434
but you don't want
a knife back here."

00:07:21.434 --> 00:07:24.300
-Yeah, Yeah.
-"I'm just a car seat lady."

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:25.934
-"You might want to --"
Yeah.

00:07:25.934 --> 00:07:28.234
Maybe don't have your kid...

00:07:28.234 --> 00:07:29.801
Yeah.
At least we're not that.

00:07:29.801 --> 00:07:31.868
-We're not that.
Neither of us had a knife there.

00:07:31.868 --> 00:07:33.534
-Hey, I think we're doing great.
-I think we're doing great.

00:07:33.534 --> 00:07:34.934
-Yeah.
-The book's fantastic.

00:07:34.934 --> 00:07:37.834
It's so lovely to see you.
You guys, that's Jessi Klein.

00:07:37.834 --> 00:07:40.601
"I'll Show Myself Out: Essays
on Midlife and Motherhood"

00:07:40.601 --> 00:07:43.067
is very funny. It's on paperback
right now.

00:07:43.067 --> 00:07:44.467
We'll be back with more
"Late Night."

